PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Split in round 0: 4 colleagues (1,4,5,8) pick C (Non-Elitism) on grounds that CLU never lets sub-critical lives outweigh any number of above-critical lives, violating the “sufficiently many modest lives beat small elite” requirement. 4 colleagues (2,3,6,9) pick D (Weak Non-Sadism) because CLU assigns negative contributive value to any w ∈ (0,c), so it can rank addition of arbitrarily many positive-welfare lives worse than addition of one negative-welfare life—precisely the Sadistic Conclusion that Weak Non-Sadism forbids. 2 colleagues (7,10) pick B (General Non-Extreme Priority) but give only loose sketches.  
Key definitional point repeated: Weak Non-Sadism = “adding lives with positive welfare cannot be strictly worse than adding lives with negative welfare.” This matches the standard axiomatic diagnosis of CLU in Arrhenius’s results; Non-Elitism and the other listed axioms are satisfiable by suitable CLU variants. No one cites E or A.  
My current belief: D is the correct answer; the C arguments appear to conflate elitism with the sadism violation. Unresolved: exact statement of each axiom in the sixth theorem—would help confirm.}

